"cite.long.prop-collapsed-list"],"evidence_ids":["mapping.long.prop-collapsed-list"],"id":"long.prop-collapsed-list","kind":"claim","label":"Long record Proposition 2.7 (Six equivalent membership conditions)","locator":"Long record Proposition 2.7, p. 29, label prop:collapsed-list","source_id":"paper.long.pdf","statement":"*Each of the following is logically *equivalent* to $1/2 \\in \\mathcal{A}$, not strictly weaker:*\n\n1.  *$C_G(N)\\le2\\sqrt N+4$ for every $N\\ge0$;*\n\n2.  *there are arbitrarily large positive exponents outside $G$;*\n\n3.  *at arbitrarily large depths $n$, some $D\\subseteq\\{2,\\ldots,n\\}$ satisfies $Q(D,n)=2^{n-1}-1$; no agreement between the sets $D$ at different depths is required;*\n\n4.  *at arbitrarily large $M$, $\\operatorname{ihc}(G,M)\\le B(M+1)$;*\n\n5.  *at arbitrarily large positive depths $M$, some $D\\subseteq\\{2,\\ldots,M\\}$ satisfies $|\\operatorname{ihc}(D,M-1)|\\le B(M)$;*\n\n6.  *the half-target greedy remainder never exceeds the full remaining tail, so no finite fatal gap exists; the seven equivalent forms in the later classification express this same condition.*\n\n*For (d) and (e), the reverse directions use the square depths in Lemma 2.6. For (a), membership gives $C_G(N)\\le2\\sqrt{N+1}+3\\le2\\sqrt N+4$ when $N\\ge1$, and $C_G(0)=0$. The forward implication still needs the greedy inequality $x_G\\le1/2$.*","status":"unverified"},"ref":"sha256:72b1b4c2b4a3766719310d7deb808e62432d8c1d3e4854b25b1285cbe6056696/nodes/long.prop-collapsed-list","relations":[{"derived":false,"family":"arguments","from":"sha256:72b1b4c2b4a3766719310d7deb808e62432d8c1d3e4854b25b1285cbe6056696/nodes/long.prop-collapsed-list","id":"sha256:72b1b4c2b4a3766719310d7deb808e62432d8c1d3e4854b25b1285cbe6056696/edges/%5B%22long.prop-collapsed-list%22%2C%22lean.ErdosProblems.Erdos257.PaperCompleteR20.six_membership_conditions%22%2C%22formalizes%22%5D","kind":"formalizes","record_ref":"sha256:72b1b4c2b4a3766719310d7deb808e62432d8c1d3e4854b25b1285cbe605